### Slide 5 — Two Writers to a Simple `int`
```cpp
static int var;

void TacheA() { int a; ... var = a; ... }
void TacheB() { int b; ... var = b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **SAFE** (against data corruption).
* **Why:** On a 32-bit/64-bit CPU, writing an aligned `int` compiles to a **single machine store instruction**. Preemption can only occur before or after that instruction, so `var` ends up holding either `a` or `b`—never half-`a`, half-`b`. Which task wins is just *instruction ordering*.

---

### Slide 6 — One Writer, One Reader on a Simple `int`
```cpp
static int var;

void taskA() { int a; ... var = a; ... }
void taskB() { int b; ... b = var; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **SAFE** (against data corruption).
* **Why:** Both `var = a` (1 store) and `b = var` (1 load) are single machine instructions. `b` reads either the old value of `var` or `a`, never a torn value.

---

### Slide 7 — Two Writers to a `struct_t`
```cpp
typedef struct { int a; int b; } struct_t;
static struct_t var;

void TacheA() { int a, b; ... var.a = a; var.b = b; ... }
void TacheB() { int a, b; ... var.a = a; var.b = b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE** (Needs mutual exclusion!).
* **Why:** Updating `var` requires **two** separate store instructions (`var.a`, then `var.b`). If `TacheA` writes `var.a`, gets preempted, `TacheB` writes both `var.a` and `var.b`, and `TacheA` resumes to write `var.b`, then `var` becomes a **torn struct**: `TacheB`'s `a` paired with `TacheA`'s `b`.

---

### Slide 8 — One Writer, One Reader on a `struct_t`
```cpp
typedef struct { int a; int b; } struct_t;
static struct_t var;

void taskA() { int a, b; ... var.a = a; var.b = b; ... }
void taskB() { int a, b; ... a = var.a; b = var.b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE** (Needs mutual exclusion!).
* **Why (Critical Exam Trap!):** Even with **only 1 writer and 1 reader**, a multi-field struct is **still unsafe**! Reading `var` takes two instructions. If `taskB` reads `a = var.a` (old), gets preempted while `taskA` updates both `var.a` and `var.b` (new), and `taskB` resumes to read `b = var.b` (new), `taskB` observes a **torn struct** `(old a, new b)` that never existed at any point in time.

### Slide 9 — Two Concurrent Increments (`var++` vs. `var++`)
```cpp
static int var;

void TacheA() { ... var++; ... }
void TacheB() { ... var++; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE** (Needs mutual exclusion!).
* **Why:** `var++` is **not** one instruction; it is a **Read-Modify-Write (RMW)** sequence of 3 instructions: `load var -> reg`, `add reg, 1`, `store reg -> var`. If `TacheA` loads `5`, gets preempted, `TacheB` loads `5`, adds `1`, and stores `6`, then `TacheA` resumes with its stale register `5`, adds `1`, and stores `6`. **Lost update!** `var` is `6` instead of `7`.

---

### Slide 10 — One Increment, One Reader (`var++` vs. `b = var`)
```cpp
static int var;

void taskA() { ... var++; ... }
void taskB() { int b; ... b = var; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **SAFE** (against data corruption on 32-bit single-core).
* **Why:** `taskB` executes a single atomic `load` (`b = var`) and **never writes** to `var`. Even if `taskB` preempts `taskA` mid-`var++`, `taskB` reads the valid pre-increment value of `var`, and `taskA` resumes to finish its increment without any lost update. *(Contrast this carefully with Slide 13 below!)*

---

### Slide 11 — Two Increments + One Reader (`var++` / `var++` / `c = var`)
```cpp
static int var = 0;

void TacheA() { ... var++; ... }
void TacheB() { ... var++; ... }
void TacheC() { int c; ... c = var; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE** (Needs mutual exclusion!).
* **Why:** Although `TacheC` (`c = var`) is a harmless single-instruction reader on a single core, `TacheA` and `TacheB` both run `var++` concurrently, reproducing the exact **lost update** race from Slide 9.

---

### Slide 12 — Check-Then-Act / TOCTOU (All 3 Tasks)
```cpp
static int var;

void taskA() { ... var++; ... }
void taskB() {
    int b;
    b = var;
    if (b > 10) {
        var = 0;
    } else {
        var++;
    }
}
void taskC() { int c; ... c = var; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE** (Needs mutual exclusion!).
* **Why:** This is a textbook **Check-Then-Act (TOCTOU — Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use)** race combined with concurrent `var++`:
  1. Suppose `var == 11`: `taskB` reads `b = 11`, passes `if (b > 10)`, and is preempted *before* `var = 0`. `taskA` runs `var++` (`var` becomes `12`). `taskB` resumes and overwrites `var = 0`—losing `taskA`'s increment.
  2. Suppose `var <= 10`: `taskB` enters `else { var++; }`, which races directly with `taskA`'s `var++` (lost update, as in Slide 9).

### Slide 14 — Concurrent `float` Writes
```cpp
static float var;

void taskA() { float a; ... var = a; ... }
void taskB() { float b; ... var = b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **Depends on architecture** — **SAFE** on 32-bit/64-bit CPUs; **UNSAFE** on 8-bit/16-bit microcontrollers.
* **Why:** An IEEE-754 `float` is **32 bits**. On a 32-bit or 64-bit bus, it transfers in a single store instruction. On an 8-bit or 16-bit embedded MCU, writing 32 bits requires 2–4 instructions, allowing a preemption mid-write to produce a **torn `float`**.

---

### Slide 15 — Concurrent `double` Writes
```cpp
static double var;

void TacheA() { double a; ... var = a; ... }
void TacheB() { double b; ... var = b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE on 32-bit (and 8/16-bit) CPUs**; SAFE only on aligned 64-bit CPUs.
* **Why:** An IEEE-754 `double` is **64 bits**. On a 32-bit CPU, storing a `double` requires **two** separate 32-bit store instructions. A context switch between them leaves `var` with 32 bits from `TacheA` and 32 bits from `TacheB`—a **torn, corrupted `double`**.

---

### Slide 16 — `std::vector<bool>` Bit-Packing Race
```cpp
std::vector<bool> vec;

void TacheA() { bool a; ... vec[0] = a; ... }
void TacheB() { bool b; ... vec[0] = b; ... }
```
* **Single-Core Verdict:** **UNSAFE!** (Needs mutual exclusion!)
* **Why:** `std::vector<bool>` is a space-optimized C++ specialization that packs **8 booleans into 1 byte** (1 bit each). CPUs cannot write a single bit directly to RAM—so `vec[0] = a` compiles to a **non-atomic read-modify-write** on the whole byte (`load byte`, `mask/set bit 0`, `store byte`).
* **Crucial Corollary (`vec[0]` vs. `vec[1]`):** Even if `TacheA` only writes `vec[0] = a` and `TacheB` only writes `vec[1] = b` (**different indices!**), they still share the **same underlying byte**, so `TacheA` can clobber `TacheB`'s bit 1 and vice versa!
